<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mikhail of Tver</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1271">1271</a>–<a href="/years/1318">1318</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Mikhail Yaroslavich</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mikhail of Tver (Mikhail Yaroslavich) was prince of Tver from 1285 and grand prince of Vladimir from 1305. His rivalry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yury-of-moscow">Yury of Moscow</a> for the favour of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> led to war; he defeated Yury in 1317 but was summoned to the Horde, tried and executed in 1318. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a> venerates him as a saint and martyr.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mikhail-of-tver.jpg" alt="Mikhail of Tver" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Тверской (Троицкий) лицевой список Хроники Георгия Амартола · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mikhail_Yaroslavich_of_Tverskoy_and_Xenia_before_Christ.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
